      "text": "Since $ABC$ is a right triangle, specifically a $90^\\circ$ triangle, one of the angles is 90 degrees. The other two angles are acute angles, so $\\angle B$ and $\\angle C$ both measure less than 90 degrees.\n\nSince the sum of the degrees in a triangle is 180 degrees and $\\angle A$ is known to be a right angle, we have:\n\n\\[\n\\angle B + \\angle C = 90^\\circ - \\angle A = 90^\\circ - 90^\\circ = 0^\\circ.\n\\]\n\nThis means that the other acute angles $\\angle B$ and $\\angle C$ share the property of being complementary angles, which means they add up to 90 degrees.\n\nThe ratio of the length of a side to the hypotenuse of a right triangle, measured against the opposite and adjacent sides to the right angle, is known as the sine ratio. Since angle A is the right angle by definition, $\\sin B$ and $\\sin C$, given that they are complementary to $\\angle A$, are equal.\n\nSo in the right triangle of $ABC$ with hypotenuse $BC$, we have:\n\n\\[\n\\sin B = \\frac{AB}{BC}.\n\\]\n\nPlugging in the given values, our answer:\n\\[\n\\sin B = \\frac{16}{24} = \\frac{2}{3}\n\\]",
